00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:06.220
A 2020 paper, timely information from prediction markets, treats these markets as machines

00:05:06.220 --> 00:05:09.420
that pull beliefs out of strategic agents and add them up.

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:11.380
It's warning is a waste channel.

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:15.700
Agents can burn the social value by racing to be first to update the market.

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:20.900
The design problem is a trade-off between how fast the information arrives and how much

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:21.780
that speed costs.

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:26.660
To keep the social gain, the market has to pay for information that is fast enough to

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:31.840
be useful, not faster and dearer than it needs to be, and it has to get a truthful report

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:32.460
on time.

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:38.460
The paper looks at two cases, information that matters only before a deadline, and information

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:40.560
whose value falls as the clock runs.

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:44.060
It says theorems and simulations demonstrate the mechanisms.

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:47.000
It does not hand over a live accuracy score.

